@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: what --credentials broker|forward actually does on the sbx backend, and who injects
' covers: internal/backend/sandbox/sandbox.go
' covers: internal/sbx/sbx.go
' covers: internal/sbx/run.go (AuthLoginArgs)
' covers: internal/run/choices.go
' covers: internal/run/params.go
' covers: internal/run/choices_test.go
' covers: internal/sbx/kit.go (Kit.Credentials, KitCredential)
' covers: internal/credentials/credentials.go (WarnMountedSecrets)
' covers: internal/sbx/secret.go (SecretSetCustomArgs, CustomSecretPlaceholder, SecretSetCustom)
'   the oauth slot proveo cannot write _spec/internal/sbx/oauth-provisioning.puml
'   and the service-name finding recorded there, NOT acted on
'   the .env guard, the refusal ...... _spec/internal/sbx/sandbox-backend.puml
'
' ── DECIDED 2026-10-16: sbx IS THE ONLY BROKER ─────────────────────────────
' The docker backend and proveo's own broker are deleted ("Retire Docker
' egress"). `--credentials forward|broker` STAYS (the Executor accepted the
' architect's recommendation); its help names sbx, not an egress layer.
' MEASURED: run.credentialsPosture returns "broker (sbx proxy)", or "forward
' (the key rides the sandbox env)" when the run forwards.
' credentials.BrokerProviders, BrokerOffReason, WriteBrokerEnv, UsableProviders,
' Without, JoinDomains and ReachableHosts are deleted.
'
' ── UPDATED 2026-09-16 (72848db): THE FORM OFFERS ONE OPTION ───────────────
' sbx is the runtime, and its proxy is where a credential belongs: stored
' host-side, substituted on the way out, never in the agent's environment. Only
' what the proxy CANNOT attach is forwarded, and the run says so.
'
'   broker    argv carries NOTHING for the credential names. proveo pushes them
'             into sbx's store host-side (`sbx secret set --force`, value on
'             stdin) and sbx's own proxy injects them as headers at egress and
'             materialises ~/.claude/.credentials.json. The agent process never
'             holds the value.
'   forward   the FORM draws both modes and GATES forward (comingSoon: "sbx's
'             proxy holds the value; forward is what that guarantee gives up").
'             The CLI flag still works. MEASURED 2026-10-16: with
'             `--credentials forward`, sandbox.Spec forwards every secret it
'             would have stored as a bare `-e NAME` (in.Forwards → addForward),
'             and the e2e launchKit helper relies on it (launchShell until
'             `--shell` was retired 2026-10-17). The posture line
'             names that route: "forward (the key rides the sandbox env)".
'
' The row is still DRAWN and gated rather than dropped. An option that vanishes
' is one an operator cannot find out about, so the form shows what is not on
' offer here and why — the same rule the locked egress row obeys.
'
' In NEITHER case does a credential VALUE appear in the argv or in the Kit.
'
' THE ONE REMAINING WAY A REAL VALUE ENTERS A SANDBOX is a provider whose
' credential the proxy cannot attach — a SigV4 signing key, a credentials FILE.
' Those still forward, and closing that hole is B4 of
' _spec/_plans/host-held-provider-keys.puml, decided and deferred.
'
' ── THE STORE ENTRY IS NAMED BY SERVICE, AND THAT WAS THE WHOLE 401 ────────
' sbx authenticates by SERVICE, so a store entry named for the ENV VAR is one
' its proxy never reads. proveo used to write `sbx.SecretSet(kv[0], …)` where
' kv[0] was the variable name — `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` — and the proxy, looking for
' `anthropic`, found nothing and attached nothing. The provider then answered 401
' to a credential that was good, and it read as a broker defect.
'
' `credentials.StoreName` now resolves the id THROUGH THE REGISTRY rather than by
' trimming `_API_KEY` off the variable, because six ids are deliberate and do not
' follow the pattern (see the naming scheme in
' _spec/cmd/proveo/init-credential-wireframe.puml).
'
' ── "broker" NAMES ONE PARTY NOW ───────────────────────────────────────────
' HISTORY: on docker, "broker" meant PROVEO's mitmproxy held the key and injected
' at the proxy. That path was deleted 2026-10-16. "broker" now means one thing:
' proveo steps aside and SBX brokers. The guarantee — the value never reaches
' the agent — is unchanged.
'
' ── WHICH KIT DECLARES A CREDENTIAL ────────────────────────────────────────
' CORRECTED 2026-10-16. This section used to say sbx.Kit had no Credentials
' field and that the KitCredential types were deleted. MEASURED: internal/sbx/
' kit.go declares `Credentials []KitCredential`, and sandbox.Spec fills it via
' agentCredentials ONLY for an own-agent Kit (`kind: sandbox`), adding each
' credential's inject domains to network.allow. A MIXIN Kit declares none: a
' mixin that repeats a service the built-in agent already declares is rejected
' outright ("defined in both"), so for a built-in agent injection policy stays
' sbx's to own.
'
' What WAS deleted, and stays deleted: sandbox.KitCredentials, an earlier
' builder with no production caller (three tests only).
'
' AND THAT IS NOW MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED, 2026-09-16. TestOpenCodeAuth/zen/
' broker PASSES with 200 where it returned 401, and TestClaudeCodeAuth/api-key/
' broker reaches the provider. So sbx's proxy injects for its BUILT-IN services
' on a MIXIN kit as soon as the store entry carries the service name. proveo
' never needed to declare `kit.Credentials`, and the section above was right for
' a better reason than it knew.
'
' WHICH RETIRES A DIAGNOSIS THAT WAS WIDELY BELIEVED, and the old reading is kept
' verbatim because it is the intuitive one and will be reached again: "Spec()
' calls agentCredentials ONLY under `if ownAgent` … the kit stays a mixin, and no
' KitCredential is ever declared for it. On the sandbox backend `--credentials
' broker` is therefore a NO-OP for every def but one." The `ownAgent` gate is
' real and still there. It was never what produced the 401.
'
' The cause was the store entry's NAME, one section up. "Naming is necessary and
' insufficient" was the conclusion drawn when a hand-stored `opencode` entry did
' not fix the run — but that test stored the entry while proveo kept writing the
' env-var-named one beside it, and the run resolved the name proveo wrote. Naming
' was necessary and SUFFICIENT; the experiment that seemed to refute it had not
' changed what proveo wrote.
'
' ── THE CUSTOM ROUTE: `sbx secret set-custom` ──────────────────────────────
' MEASURED in code: Spec's secret loop asks customSecretTarget first, and a
' provider outside sbx.BuiltinServices goes to `sbx secret set-custom --host
' <pattern>… --env <VAR>` instead of `sbx secret set` (sbx.SecretSetCustom).
' Two rules hold on that route:
'   THE VALUE NEVER RIDES argv. `--value` and `--token` exist, and sbx's help
'     calls both "less secure: visible in shell history"; argv is also visible
'     to anyone running ps, and the suite asserts no provider key appears
'     there. The value goes on stdin, as `secret set` already passes one.
'   REPLACE, NEVER ADD. set-custom has no --force and REFUSES a variable it
'     already holds ("custom secret env … already exists in scope"), so a
'     second run of the same def died before its shell. SecretSetCustom finds
'     the old entry's placeholder and removes it first: the placeholder is the
'     only handle `sbx secret rm` takes for a custom secret.
' OPEN, escalated 2026-10-17 rather than reconciled (N14): WHY the route
' exists. The section above calls a service-named `opencode` entry SUFFICIENT;
' customSecretTarget's doc and secret_test.go record it answering 401.
' Ruled 2026-10-17: re-measure live before reconciling; until then those
' comments stay whole in source.
'
' ── THE STORE IS HOST-WIDE, AND THAT IS THE HAZARD ─────────────────────────
' `sbx secret set --force` writes to a store shared by EVERY sandbox on the host,
' and the entry outlives the run. A stale entry from an earlier run can therefore
' authenticate a later one that never chose it — which reads as proveo picking a
' credential the operator did not, somewhere they cannot see. A run that writes
' says so once, naming `sbx secret ls`.
'
' Same shape as the policy baseline: a per-run choice with a host-global effect.
' The difference is that proveo DOES write here, because it must — the credential
' cannot reach sbx's proxy any other way — whereas it only ever reports the
' baseline.
'
' ── THE WORKSPACE .env: READABLE, NEVER LOADED (CORRECTED 2026-10-16) ──────
' HISTORY: sbx takes directory workspaces, so the repo's .env* files are mounted
' whole, and the image skipped sourcing them only when PROVEO_EGRESS_MODE named
' a tier. When this section was written the Kit set no tier, so the prelude
' sourced the .env with `set -a` and every key became agent environment.
'
' MEASURED now (N7, 2026-10-17): the image reads no workspace .env at all —
' load_env, find_env_file, ShouldSkipEnvLoad and LoadEnvFile are deleted, so
' the SANDBOX_NAME / SANDBOX_VM_ID guard (2026-10-16) went with them. The
' project .env's NON-PROVIDER values reach a NEW sandbox from the host through
' `--env-file` (seedProjectEnv, internal/backend/sandbox/project_env.go): it
' drops every provider.KeyVars name unless the run forwards credentials, so on
' the default broker path no provider key enters the agent env this way; on
' `--credentials forward` the .env's keys travel like the forwarded ones
' (_spec/internal/credentials/credential-decisions.puml, PROJECT .env). The
' FILE stays readable in a mounted checkout, and WarnMountedSecrets(dir, lookup)
' warns when it holds a provider key: "the agent can read the file (the image
' never loads it into the agent's environment)".
'
' ── D1, RESOLVED ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' proveo used to emit `-e VAR=` (empty) on the theory that sbx's global store
' would otherwise inject a stale value in front of a mounted login. Probed against
' sbx v0.39.0 with both keys in the store and no -e flag: both arrive UNSET. sbx
' does not export service secrets as environment variables. The empty value was
' not merely useless — an agent reads a SET variable as a chosen credential
' whatever its value, so a blank one occupied the slot the login needed. Removed.
title --credentials on sbx — who holds the key, and who injects it

participant "operator" as OP <<app>>
participant "proveo" as PV <<app>>
participant "sbx secret store\nHOST-WIDE" as STORE <<data>>
participant "sbx proxy" as PROXY <<cloud>>
participant "agent\nin the sandbox" as AG <<app>>

OP ARROW_MAIN PV : --credentials broker (default)
PV ARROW_MAIN STORE : sbx secret set --force NAME (stdin)
STORE ARROW_QUEUE PROXY : proxy-managed
PROXY ARROW_MAIN AG : header injected at egress;\n.credentials.json materialised
PV ARROW_OPTIONAL AG : argv carries NO name and NO value

OP ARROW_MAIN PV : --credentials forward
PV ARROW_ERROR AG : bare `-e NAME` — the host value\nlands in the agent's environment

note over STORE
  **Shared, and it persists.** Every sandbox on
  the machine sees this store, and ""--force""
  overwrites. What one run writes can decide a
  later run's identity. A run that writes says
  so once and names ""sbx secret ls"".
end note

note over PV
  **Only an own-agent Kit declares credentials.**
  A mixin repeating a built-in agent's service
  is rejected, so it declares none; a ""kind:
  sandbox"" Kit fills ""Kit.Credentials"".
end note

note left of PV
  Broker always stands, default or
  named: sbx's proxy brokers cursor
  natively, and no manifest pins
  forward since cursor's pins were
  dropped on 2026-10-16.
end note

note right of PV
  **The form gates forward; the flag does
  not.** ""--credentials forward"" still puts
  the value in the agent env, and the posture
  line says so.
end note
@enduml
